answer_text:
No. The host per-device “Memory used” chart is not evidence that the workloads are using GPU memory.

On an ESXi host, the per-GPU “Memory used” counter shows the vGPU frame buffer allocated to powered-on vGPU VMs. NVIDIA vGPU allocates the whole profile frame buffer when the VM powers on and frees it when it powers off. In that host chart, “used” means allocation, not active workload use. High values of 94% and 88% with compute usage below 5% are expected if the six vGPU VMs are powered on and their profiles fill most of each GPU, even when the notebooks are idle. Low compute use does not free frame buffer; vGPU frame buffer is not overcommitted.

To read what the workloads actually use, use the cluster or resource pool GPU counters, which are level 1 in vCenter 9.1:
- gpu.mem.used: frame buffer touched by workloads;
- gpu.mem.reserved: sum of vGPU profile frame buffers;
- gpu.mem.total: total GPU memory;
- gpu.utilization: compute use.

On vSphere 9.0/9.1, gpu.mem.used is at or below gpu.mem.reserved. If gpu.mem.used is low while gpu.mem.reserved is high, the GPUs are reserved, not busy.

Before buying GPUs, check gpu.mem.used, gpu.mem.reserved, gpu.mem.total, gpu.utilization, and DirectPath Profiles Remaining per profile. Placement can fail because of per-GPU profile fragmentation, not because cluster memory is full.
